WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.350
Many threat hunts begin with a hypothesis: a structured,

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.560
testable assumption that guides the investigation.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.520
A useful hypothesis explains what the team is concerned about,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.541
why the concern is reasonable, and what evidence would help evaluate it.

00:00:12.541 --> 00:00:16.150
Hypotheses may come from threat intelligence, incident lessons, environmental changes,

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.715
detection gaps, risk assessments, unusual trends, or questions raised by system owners.

00:00:20.715 --> 00:00:23.235
The source should connect the hunt to an organizational need.

00:00:23.235 --> 00:00:26.070
A well-formed hypothesis is narrow enough to investigate.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.670
It identifies the relevant environment, expected behavior, time period,

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:33.320
and data sources without assuming that malicious activity has already occurred.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.185
Before analysis begins, hunters should define scope and success criteria.

00:00:37.185 --> 00:00:40.125
They should know which systems are included, which data is available,

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:43.775
what limitations exist, and what observations would support, reject,

00:00:43.775 --> 00:00:45.220
or refine the hypothesis.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:49.650
Baseline knowledge is important because unusual does not automatically mean malicious.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.130
Maintenance, software deployment, administrative work, business travel, automation,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.960
and emergency operations can all create uncommon but legitimate patterns.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.361
As evidence develops, the hypothesis may change.

00:00:59.361 --> 00:01:02.950
A disciplined hunter follows the data, records alternative explanations,

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:06.020
and avoids forcing observations into the original theory.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.560
This reduces confirmation bias and improves analytical credibility.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.475
The hunt record should capture the question, rationale, scope,

00:01:12.475 --> 00:01:16.765
queries or analytical methods at an appropriate level, evidence reviewed, limitations,

00:01:16.765 --> 00:01:18.910
conclusion, and recommended follow-up.

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:21.240
Documentation makes the work repeatable and teachable.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.525
A conclusion may identify suspicious activity,

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:26.943
reject the concern within the available evidence, or remain inconclusive.

00:01:26.943 --> 00:01:30.810
Each outcome can still reveal useful improvements to data, detections, processes,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.280
and future hunting questions.
